          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Karl Benz</strong><br>\nKarl Benz was born on November 25, 1844, in Karlsruhe, Germany. His father, an engine driver, passed away when Karl was just two years old, leaving his family in financial hardship. Despite these challenges, Benz excelled in school, showing a keen interest in science and engineering. His mother's support allowed him to pursue his studies, eventually leading him to the University of Karlsruhe, where he studied mechanical engineering.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Benz's First Innovations</strong><br>\nAfter completing his education, Benz focused on designing stationary engines. He worked at various jobs before successfully designing his first engine in 1878. This invention was a significant breakthrough for Benz, as it allowed him to open his own company, producing industrial machines. His first patented product was a gas-powered engine in 1879, a vital step toward his later work with automobiles.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Birth of the Automobile</strong><br>\nIn 1885, Benz created the Motorwagen, which is considered the first practical automobile. It had three wheels, a gas-powered engine, and was capable of transporting a person. His design incorporated an innovative electrical ignition system, differential gears, and water cooling. This invention marked a significant milestone in the development of modern transportation.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
