          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Patents and Progress</strong><br>\nKarl Benz was granted a patent for his Motorwagen on January 29, 1886. He started Benz &amp; Company to produce his vehicles on a larger scale. The following years were filled with improvements and new models, including the introduction of the four-stroke engine and the first car with a horizontally opposed piston.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Growth of Benz &amp; Company</strong><br>\nUnder Karl Benz's guidance, Benz &amp; Company grew rapidly, becoming one of the largest automobile companies of the era. Benz introduced several new models and made constant improvements to the design and mechanics of his cars. His wife, Bertha Benz, played a crucial role in demonstrating the potential of the automobile by undertaking a long-distance trip in one of his cars, which garnered public attention.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Global Impact of Benz's Inventions</strong><br>\nThe inventions of Karl Benz had a global impact, leading to the widespread adoption of automobiles. Cities began to change, with roads being built to accommodate cars, and industries emerged to support the growing demand for vehicles and related services. The automotive industry sparked economic growth and transformed transportation worldwide.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
