          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Late Career and Honors</strong><br>\nIn his later career, Benz continued to innovate, focusing on safety and comfort in his automobile designs. He received numerous honors for his contributions to engineering and transportation. His legacy includes the recognition as one of the principal founders of the automotive industry, with his name living on in the Mercedes-Benz brand.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nKarl Benz was married to Bertha Ringer, and they had five children. Bertha's support was crucial to his success, not just personally but professionally, as she financed his work and marketed his inventions. Karl Benz died on April 4, 1929, leaving behind a legacy that had forever changed the way people travel. His innovations laid the foundation for future automotive technology.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Benz's Posthumous Influence</strong><br>\nAfter his death, Karl Benz's company merged with Daimler-Motoren-Gesellschaft in 1926, forming the brand now known as Mercedes-Benz. The brand carries his legacy forward, continually shaping the automobile industry. Benz is remembered not only as an inventor and engineer but also as a pioneer who realized the dream of personal transportation.</p>\n"
              },
